With streaming synthesis enabled, the time to first playable audio drops below 800 milliseconds.
The VAD model split this recording into three speech segments and dropped the silent parts.
After int8 quantization, Kokoro v1.1 ships at roughly 320 MB and Mandarin naturalness is close to usable.
SenseVoice's automatic language detection is not very stable on Cantonese-Mandarin code-switched speech.
The realtime mode runs through argos with end-to-end latency around 200 ms, fast enough for face-to-face scenarios.
The quality mode chains madlad and ollama and prioritizes accuracy over a latency budget.
Privacy mode is a hard invariant: no configuration can route cloud engines into the pipeline.
Once the translation memory hit rate passes thirty percent, cost is essentially a one-time model expense.
A buffer that is too small causes stuttering; one that is too large inflates end-to-end latency.
The sample rate and bit depth of audio determine the volume of raw waveform data.
Mono 16 kHz 16-bit is the most common input format for automatic speech recognition.
Stereo dual-channel audio is usually down-mixed to mono before being fed to the ASR model in a dubbing workflow.
A frame size of 25 ms and a frame shift of 10 ms is the standard setup for acoustic feature extraction.
The mel spectrogram is the key intermediate representation that turns a waveform into something the model can read.
Forced alignment lines up the reference text and the recognition output word by word, used to generate timestamps.
Inverse text normalization converts "two thousand twenty-six" into "2026" so downstream parsing is simpler.
In code-switched sentences, domain terminology should be pulled from the glossary rather than left to the model's imagination.
Prompt injection into Ollama must explicitly disable thinking mode, otherwise the time to first token doubles.
The CUDA provider gives TTS roughly a three-times speedup on this RTX 4050.
Running a 3B int8 model on CPU is realistically 2 to 4 seconds per sentence, not fit for realtime scenarios.
The subtitle track exported as SRT can be read directly by players without re-encoding the video.
Bilingual subtitles require timing alignment: the Chinese and English lines must share the same time window.
Burned-in subtitles are part of the picture, so editing them later requires re-encoding; soft subtitles can be swapped at any time.
Dubbing and lip sync are separate concerns: the first replaces the audio, the second keeps the picture in sync.
Audio-video sync error within 40 milliseconds is imperceptible to human viewers.
A higher quantization parameter means a smaller bitrate but worse quality; the trade-off has to be tuned.
A longer GOP lowers bitrate but makes seek response sluggish; live streaming usually caps it at two seconds.
Word error rate and character error rate are not interchangeable; Chinese benchmarks typically report character error rate.
FLORES-101 is general domain, still a distributional mismatch with the confidential-document and batch-localization scenarios this product targets.
The glossary is looked up by language pair; entries with the same source term collide between generic and domain rows, so the primary key needs a domain segment.
Lowering the voice activity detection threshold cuts breathing into speech segments as well; raising it swallows weak syllables at sentence ends.
Post-processing for automatic speech recognition usually involves two independent steps: punctuation restoration and number normalization.
In neural machine translation the encoder squeezes the source sentence into a context vector and the decoder autoregressively generates the target sentence.
The text-to-speech frontend runs three stages -- tokenization, grapheme-to-phoneme conversion and prosody prediction -- and a failure in any of them hurts naturalness.
Streaming recognition differs from offline recognition not only in latency but also in the consistency between prefix hypotheses and the final result.
The most common trade-off when productizing real-time interpretation is "buying accuracy with latency", and the sliding-window size directly determines both.
A batch localization pipeline must survive a thousand-sentence run for hours without crashing, which makes checkpoint-resume and idempotent retries hard requirements.
Speech translation and text translation share the same routing: TM hit, privacy clamp, per-language-pair tiering, confidence gating and AI upgrade.
Endpoint detection is stricter than VAD -- it has to distinguish "sentence finished" from "pause inside a sentence".
Speaker diarization works well on meeting recordings but degrades visibly on podcasts with overlapping speech.
Expressive speech synthesis needs an extra prosody conditioning channel and cannot rely on the text frontend alone.
Zero-shot speaker cloning can start from just five seconds of reference audio but is sensitive to sample rate and signal-to-noise ratio.
A speech recognition confidence score cannot be lifted directly into a translation confidence score; their calibration curves are entirely different.
Among Kokoro's Mandarin voices at sid 45-52, 52 handles CJK/Latin code-switching best while 48 sits closer to a broadcast tone.
Whisper-style models drift on long audio, so the standard slicing policy follows VAD boundaries rather than a fixed 30 seconds.
The causality constraint in streaming ASR means the current hypothesis cannot look ahead to future frames -- a fundamental difference from offline models.
Real-time subtitles usually stabilize the prefix and let the suffix float, so a single sentence is not rewritten three times for the reader.
Batch localization jobs often run overnight, so log aggregation and assertion-style failures must be replayable after the fact.
End-to-end speech translation collapses ASR, MT and TTS into a single model, trading interpretability for lower latency.
The audio sample rate and bit depth set the Nyquist ceiling; 16 kHz speech can only carry content below 8 kHz.
A vocoder inverts the spectrum back into a waveform -- Griffin-Lim is slow and muddy, while neural vocoders are fast and clean.
Fundamental-frequency estimation usually uses autocorrelation or YIN; both are accurate on voiced segments, but unvoiced segments are noise by definition.
The first 40 cepstral coefficients already cover most of the spectral detail the human ear can distinguish.
Down-mixing stereo to mono should normalize by energy rather than average samples, otherwise the phase difference between channels colors the output.
Silence compression keeps only the segments VAD flags as speech, saving 30 to 50 percent of the length on typical meeting recordings.
Resampling from 48 kHz to 16 kHz must apply an anti-aliasing low-pass filter first, otherwise high frequencies fold into the speech band.
Field recordings with a signal-to-noise ratio below 10 dB usually need denoising before being sent to ASR.
Pre-emphasis filtering lifts high frequencies so that later feature extraction is more sensitive to weak fricatives.
A Hamming window is standard during framing and noticeably reduces the Gibbs ringing a rectangular window would introduce.
A 25 fps frame rate is normal for film; 50 fps makes viewers feel it "looks too much like news footage".
Raising bit depth from 16 to 24 does not increase loudness but improves dynamic range and quantization noise noticeably.
Audio-video sync has to leave a small buffer skew in the encoding chain -- video is typically about 20 ms behind audio.
Down-mixing from 5.1 to stereo should use Dolby Pro Logic rather than a straight sum.
Spectral centroid, zero-crossing rate and energy are enough to separate music from speech.
The mel scale mirrors the non-linear frequency perception of human hearing; a linear FFT binning does not match psychoacoustics.
Pitch period and formants are the two most basic concepts in the physical model of speech production.
Voiceprint recognition and speaker diarization are not the same: the first identifies a fixed set of speakers, the second only clusters without identities.
Insufficient audio buffering causes dropouts, insufficient video buffering causes frame skips -- different symptoms but the same root cause is backpressure.
In streaming protocols chunk and packet are often used interchangeably, but for latency accounting they are two different concepts.
A jitter buffer dynamically retargets delay -- pushing it down to 20 ms when the network is good and stretching it to 200 ms when the network is bad.
Any throughput bottleneck in a pipeline will back up everything upstream, so the backpressure signal has to propagate all the way to the input.
TCP retransmission ruins real-time behaviour; low-latency audio and video usually ride on UDP with forward error correction.
Keyframes spaced too far apart make seeking stutter; too close and the bitrate doubles for the same quality.
Chunked processing needs 50 percent overlap between adjacent chunks, otherwise the energy estimate jumps at the boundaries.
Flush means "emit everything currently in the buffer and terminate this segment".
If backpressure is not designed in an end-to-end pipeline, a slow consumer at the tail stalls the entire chain.
Buffer prefetch usually counts in frames, not in seconds, because a frame is the atomic unit of the model.
Streaming synthesis boundaries should land after a word or phrase; cutting at a particle or conjunction is audibly jarring.
When the datagram transport protocol drops one or two packets, the decoder should gracefully conceal rather than crash.
Constant bitrate suits broadcast, variable bitrate suits storage -- their buffering models are completely different.
The container only handles packaging and synchronisation; the codec handles compression. Turning an mkv into an mp4 needs no re-encoding.
The network abstraction layer of the bitstream carries timing and composition information; missing it leaves the decoder unable to render correctly.
The encoder must declare its profile and level explicitly so the player can decide whether it can accept this stream.
Demultiplexing means splitting a container into its video, audio and subtitle tracks without decoding anything.
Lossless compression entropy-codes the waveform and can restore it bit-for-bit; lossy compression introduces quantization error.
Transcoding between different source and target codecs must go through a frame buffer for reorder.
Intra-frame prediction depends only on the current frame; inter-frame prediction references other frames, and the two propagate errors differently.
A larger motion-estimation search radius is more accurate but slower; 16 pixels is the common default.
Entropy coding uses Huffman or arithmetic coding -- the latter is slightly more efficient but carries patent and complexity overhead.
Rate control should be content-aware: lower bitrate for static scenes, higher bitrate for motion-heavy scenes.
Each generation from AVC to HEVC to VVC roughly halves the bitrate for the same quality but doubles the complexity.
Opus adapts between 6 and 512 kbps and is the default choice for voice on the internet.
FLAC compresses losslessly around 5:1 and is suited to archiving masters; MP3 is lossy and should never be secondarily compressed.
Video subtitles have to handle three independent problems: line-width wrapping, character encoding and missing glyphs.
SubRip only has timing and text fields, weak styling; ASS adds event and style sections and can express karaoke effects.
WebVTT is the W3C-defined subtitle format for the web and slots straight into HTML5 players.
Word-level timestamps require forced-alignment output; ASR alone only provides segment-level timing.
Subtitle line breaks should follow meaning rather than character count; splitting "audio-video sync" across two lines wrecks readability.
Burning in subtitles means extracting frames, drawing captions and re-encoding -- each step can add a generation of loss.
Lip-sync algorithms track mouth keypoints on real people and match frame-by-frame energy envelopes on animation.
When dubbing, duck the original audio by 12 dB or viewers will miss the new voice.
Voiceover scenes usually keep a little background ambience; pure silence makes the viewer drop out of the scene.
Multi-role dubbing must synthesise each speaker separately by diarization result, then remux by timestamp.
When bilingual subtitles have mismatched segment counts, split segments rather than let them drift out of phase.
Timeline drift accumulates on long videos; check the anchors by hand every ten minutes.
Extracting an audio track usually uses ffmpeg's -vn flag; the cost is remuxing, not re-encoding.
Embedding subtitles into the video stream lets even non-subtitle players display them, at the cost of losing the on/off toggle.
Remuxing a video where the codec has not changed can copy packet-by-packet and finish in seconds.
Frame-accurate subtitle alignment requires millisecond timestamps; segment-level accuracy is not enough for fast speech.
SMPTE 12-hour timecode has drop-frame and non-drop-frame variants at 30 fps; state which one when synchronising across systems.
Dubbing scripts must match the duration of the original video; when writing translations, estimate the syllable budget from visible mouth openness.
A model checkpoint should store weights, optimiser state and the tokenizer together, so resuming training and inference are both easy.
Subword tokenization breaks unknown words into combinable pieces; vocabulary size and merge granularity are directly related.
Byte-pair encoding starts from characters and repeatedly merges the most frequent pairs, producing a vocabulary optimised for that corpus.
Word embeddings map symbols into continuous space so that semantically similar words lie close together in the vector space.
Attention allows the decoder at every step to softly inspect every position in the source sentence.
The Transformer replaces recurrence with self-attention -- high parallelism during training, but KV-cache cost at inference time.
Quantising to int8 shrinks the model to about a third of its size; the speed gain depends on hardware and operators.
Half-precision fp16 keeps the dynamic range of floats and is used heavily in training; int8 is used heavily in inference.
Knowledge distillation feeds the soft labels of a large model into a small model so the latter approaches the former's accuracy.
The teacher might have 30B parameters and the student only 1B; a distilled student loses surprisingly little on a narrow task.
Fine-tuning continues training on a domain-specific corpus at a much lower learning rate than pretraining.
LoRA only trains two low-rank matrices, less than one percent of the base model's parameter count.
At inference time, larger batch size raises throughput but also latency; realtime pipelines usually lock batch=1.
More threads are not always better; ONNX's inter-op and intra-op thread counts have to be tuned separately.
Operator fusion collapses several small operators into one, reducing memory round-trips and scheduling overhead.
Computation-graph optimisation is the core work of an inference framework; graph partitioning decides which operators stay on CPU and which move to GPU.
Choosing the runtime provider matters a lot -- the same model can be 5 to 10 times faster on CUDA than on CPU.
When VRAM is insufficient Ollama offloads some layers to main memory; throughput collapses but the process survives.
The product of batch size and sequence length determines the VRAM cost of the KV cache.
A multilingual model covers more than a hundred languages with one set of weights, and quality on cold pairs is usually much lower than on hot pairs.
The tokenizer and the model's vocabulary must match exactly; a mismatch produces garbled output without raising an error.
Temperature sampling flattens the probability distribution and yields more varied output, but translation typically wants lower temperature.
The context window is a hard constraint on the LLM; long documents must be truncated or fed one sentence at a time.
Listing the glossary explicitly inside the system prompt significantly reduces terminology errors but consumes input tokens.
Few-shot examples put the user's preferred rendering directly in front of the model, which beats any abstract instruction.
Glossaries must be filtered by the current request's language pair and domain; feeding irrelevant rows only dilutes attention.
Translation memory matches exactly -- a hit is zero-cost; fuzzy matching is a different problem and brings ranking noise.
Confidence gating is the switch between local output and AI upgrade; too permissive a threshold wastes API calls.
The quality score records each TM entry's historical reliability -- AI writes at 0.95, local estimates its own, manual writes 1.0.
Fallback degradation must carry a reason so the caller can tell "could not translate" apart from "translated wrong but degraded anyway".
No silent degradation is this product's hard constraint: every fallback must be written into the message field.
Distillation extracts terms from AI quality translations and feeds them back into local engines, forming a "cheaper the more you use it" flywheel.
Hit rate and quality score fight each other -- repeatedly hitting a low-quality memory pair poisons the output.
The LRU cache should reserve a hit-rate threshold as the warm-up signal; the cold-start phase must not evict too aggressively.
End-to-end evaluation samples must cover three scenarios: perfect hit, cache-quality-refused re-translation, and cold translation without cache.
Neural machine translation is extremely sensitive to the training domain -- a model trained on Wikipedia drops heavily when used on meeting transcripts.
A large language model can translate proper nouns in a cold start using world knowledge, but without term injection the same term can drift across a long document.
Statistical machine translation relies on phrase tables and aligned corpora; harder to maintain in production but latency is predictable.
The real-time factor RTF is the model's compute cost divided by the audio length; only RTF below 1 makes realtime feasible.
Time-to-first-token decides the user's gut feeling of "fast"; total time decides whether they can "wait".
Wall-clock time is measured end-to-end and includes queuing and network round-trips; it is not the same as CPU time.
The first inference during a cold start loads weights and builds the computation graph, taking an order of magnitude longer than steady state.
Warmup usually fires a few synthetic requests to bring weights from disk into memory and to trigger lazy graph optimisation.
Memory footprint is a hard constraint for desktop products -- users will accept CPU slowness but not OOM.
Queries per second is a server metric and says little about a single-machine desktop product.
Profiling must separate inference time, codec time and I/O time, otherwise optimisation ends up targeting the wrong thing.
End-to-end throughput is capped by the slowest stage; find the bottleneck before optimising.
Benchmarks must pin the seed and the sampling policy, otherwise two runs on the same model are not comparable.
Latency measurements are ruined by concurrent downloads or antivirus scans; run them on an idle machine.
In AV context a buffer is an audio buffer; in chemistry it is a buffer solution.
A stream in streaming synthesis is a data stream; in statistics it is a data stream too, but in geography it is a brook.
A channel in audio is a channel; in a message queue it is a channel; in marketing it is also a channel -- different meanings.
A segment in VAD is a speech segment; in business it is a market segment; in geometry it is a line segment.
In TTS, voice most often means the speaker's timbre, not the physical sense of sound.
Speech emphasises spoken language and the acoustic signal; voice leans toward the voice quality and the speaking subject.
Frame means frame in the codec context and also frame in the picture/window context -- only surrounding text disambiguates.
Sample in audio is a discrete sample; in statistics it is a sampled unit -- the English word is the same.
Model in machine learning is a trained predictor; in fashion or scale cars it is also a model.
Pitch in speech is fundamental frequency; in baseball it is a throw; in business it is a proposal.
Prosody appears only in phonetics -- a pure domain anchor.
Vocoder is unique to speech engineering ("声码器"); most users will not recognise it, so the UI has to explain.
Diarization means speaker separation; combined with recognition it answers "who said what when".
Alignment in subtitle context is temporal alignment; in NLP it is cross-lingual alignment; in geometry it is arrangement.
Decoder is a hardware or software component in the codec sense, and also the second half of an NMT model -- related meaning, different implementation.
Encoder and decoder are symmetric; in neural networks both share the name, but in engineering one may be software and the other hardware.
A codec is encoder plus decoder fused; the word is usually restricted to the compression domain.
In video engineering a container is a container format (mkv/mp4); in software deployment a container is a Docker container.
In real-time audio and video, latency is the sum of network and decoding delays; in computer architecture it is pipeline latency.
Jitter means network jitter in one context and clock jitter in another -- the mechanisms differ and translation must separate them.
Throughput and bandwidth are often conflated; the first is achieved work, the second is the theoretical ceiling.
Chunk, packet and frame are three different granularities in streaming media and cannot all be translated as "块".
In the LLM domain a prompt is an instruction to the model; in the OS shell it is a command prompt -- entirely different meanings.
A token in NLP is a subword unit; in authentication it is an access token.
Transcode re-encodes; remux only rewraps. Their quality loss profiles differ fundamentally.
Burn-in hard-codes subtitles into the pixels; overlay draws them at render time. They sit at different stages of the pipeline.
When recording, check microphone gain before system volume -- clipping at the input cannot be undone by any algorithm.
Switching the output speaker on Windows rebuilds the audio graph, and many applications drop a frame during the change.
Dragging the playhead should explicitly seek to a keyframe; otherwise viewers see a block of corrupted frames during the transition.
The preview pane renders soft subtitles by default; hard-burn is an explicit export option.
The timeline panel supports frame-level nudge -- left and right arrows step exactly one frame.
Cut silence before exporting an edit, otherwise the final piece has meaningless gaps.
When importing AV footage, explicitly pick the frame-rate interpretation policy -- mixed-source clips drop frames otherwise.
The waveform view lets the user hand-tune VAD boundaries, which is invaluable when the auto-segmentation mis-cuts.
When exporting bilingual subtitles, keep independent timestamps for each track -- sharing one drifts fast.
On machines with high preview latency, loosen the VAD threshold so users see the rough segmentation before refining.
Detect sample rate and bit depth on import; if they mismatch, offer a resampling choice instead of silently converting.
The local engine matrix covers three real engines -- argos, madlad and ollama -- plus a built-in demo as the last fallback.
The sidecar binds only to 127.0.0.1 to enforce the privacy invariant: no external process can touch this machine's translation stream.
Language-pair tiering decides which engine runs; some cold pairs have no local engine at all.
When delivering the offline bundle, ship weights, vocabularies and configuration together -- the user's first launch should not need network access.
Quality mode and realtime mode differ not in speed but in budget: one permits AI upgrade, the other does not.
The quality-preference toggle steers the upgrade policy of quality mode, choosing between "quality first" and "cost first".
Fallback states explicitly that "the local model for this pair is missing", prompting the user to download rather than assume the product is broken.
Every layer of the translation pipeline is observable: TM hit, privacy clamp, language-pair tiering, local engine, AI upgrade and confidence gating.
The model-licence matrix is part of the outward commitment -- Silero MIT, SenseVoice MIT, Kokoro Apache-2.0 and Argos MIT all clear.
Execution providers are tiered by operating system: Windows prefers CUDA or DirectML, macOS prefers CoreML, Linux prefers CUDA.
The training target of neural machine translation is to minimise cross-entropy, which only partially aligns with the human intuition of "faithfulness, expressiveness, elegance".
Translation memory and glossary solve different problems: the first reuses whole-sentence history, the second constrains word-level rendering.
Confidence should be bound to the translation itself, not to the engine -- the same model can drift across batches.
A fallback path must preserve request context so the next layer can see why the previous one did not run.
No-silent-degradation requires the response message to carry the full causal chain so the caller can decide to retry or prompt the user.
The context window is a hard constraint on the LLM; long documents must be truncated or fed one sentence at a time, never stuffed whole.
More few-shot examples are not always better -- five high-quality examples often beat twenty low-quality ones.
The distillation store threshold determines glossary signal-to-noise; too permissive lets wrong terms pollute every subsequent request.
Term injection into the prompt must specify format explicitly, otherwise the model reads the Chinese terms as explanatory prose.
A multilingual model's performance on cold pairs is bound by training corpus size, not model capacity.
Neural machine translation's tokenizer is bound to its training corpus; using it on a different corpus makes the vocabulary hit rate collapse.
A large language model can do machine translation but is sensitive to prompt engineering -- the same source sentence can differ by a tier depending on the prompt.
Automatic evaluation cannot replace human evaluation; on narrow domains the correlation between chrF/BLEU/COMET and human judgement drops noticeably.
Phrase tables in statistical machine translation degrade badly for pairs with large word-order gaps, e.g. Chinese to Finnish.
Fuzzy match over translation memory sorts by edit distance or semantic distance; the returned top-k must stay small to avoid noise.
Confidence gating has to be stricter in realtime mode because realtime does not allow upgrade, and looser in quality mode because AI provides a floor.
Term injection behaves differently on local engines versus LLMs: local treats it as a hard constraint, LLMs integrate it into context before deciding.
Distillation output must distinguish "user confirmed" from "machine extracted"; the first can overwrite the second, not the other way round.
Hit rate and latency are usually positively correlated -- a higher hit rate means more repeated content and faster cached paths.
In end-to-end speech translation, ASR errors propagate into MT and then into TTS, so segment-level evaluation has to run independently.
Attention visualisation reveals which part of the source the model "looked at" but is not the same as interpretability.
Whether translation memory uses last-write-wins or best-quality-wins yields entirely different product semantics.
Prompt injection is one source of privacy risk for LLMs; glossary content must never include executable instructions.
A translation engine's fallback is not a simple "return original on failure" -- it must state why the translation did not happen.
End-to-end evaluation samples must cover short, medium and long sentences -- short polysemy and long complex sentences fail in different ways.
User-locked entries have the highest priority: neither distillation nor seed-pack imports may overwrite them unless the user explicitly unlocks them.
Microphone gain that is too high produces clipped waveforms with no recognisable content and degrades ASR visibly.
Loudness normalisation for loudspeaker output must happen before encoding, not after decoding, or the dynamic range is lost.
Silence trimming must follow VAD decisions rather than a naive energy threshold on the raw waveform.
Cross-segment playhead jumps must trigger a flush, otherwise the previous segment's audio stays in the buffer.
The timeline panel must distinguish video frame indices from audio sample indices; converting between them depends on frame rate and sample rate.
Importing a subtitle file that contains a BOM must detect and strip it, or the first line carries an invisible character.
SRT export needs millisecond precision; ASS uses centiseconds -- the two formats are not interchangeable.
The preview pane renders soft subtitles so users can toggle tracks; the export option must stay decoupled from this choice.
When exporting an edit that only changes timing and not content, remuxing is enough -- do not introduce unnecessary re-encoding.
Waveform and spectrogram views are complementary: the first shows energy distribution, the second shows frequency distribution.
Beam-forming on a microphone array markedly improves signal-to-noise for distant speakers, provided sample sync is preserved.
Before export, verify sample rate, channels and bit depth in one pass and prompt on mismatch.
Audio format conversion must let the caller choose whether to resample, and resampling must declare whether to use a high-quality filter.
Every outward-published number in this project must trace back to a measured run; estimates are labelled "estimated" without exception.
The sidecar binds only to the local loopback, and any external exposure would violate the privacy invariant explicitly.
Language-pair tiering decides which engine runs; cold pairs may not have any local engine at all.
When delivering an offline bundle the customer environment might be an air-gapped network, so neither models nor vocabularies may rely on runtime download.
Quality mode promises "escalate to AI when reachable", not "stay local forever".
The latency budget in realtime mode is a hard constraint; anything that would break it, AI upgrade included, must be explicitly disabled.
The quality-preference toggle is a two-way choice in settings; changes take effect only on restart so routing cannot mutate mid-flight.
Fallback must distinguish "local model missing" from "external engine unreachable"; the first invites a download, the second a retry.
The model-licence matrix is part of the outward commitment; every new dependency goes through the licence gate.
Execution-provider selection must go through environment variables, never hard-coded in source.
Routing is observable: every translation exposes its route_plan over the diagnostics interface.
Local engines and cloud engines are a hard boundary; privacy mode closes every path across that boundary.
The translation pipeline is data-driven: routes can be edited, and any unknown configuration key is echoed back rather than silently dropped.
Confidence estimation is not a raw model probability; it is a synthesis of quality heuristics, and the product says so.
The end-to-end speech loop caps around 500 MB of model weight; anything larger is unfit for a first-launch download.
Real-time interpretation products in the demo phase say "transcription plus translation"; promising "interpretation" is a narrative-discipline violation.
A batch localization pipeline should be able to import mp4, extract audio, run ASR, translate, and write soft subtitles back -- all four steps interruptible and resumable.
Confidential-document scenarios require every local component to be obtainable offline; network features must be globally disableable.
The desktop client shows which engine actually ran each translation -- transparent traceability is a baseline promise of this product.
Translation memory exports must be portable and shareable; but glossary seed packs must not carry user-private entries.
End-to-end latency is the sum of VAD, ASR, MT and TTS; degradation in any one of them lands squarely on the user's face.
Batch job logs must persist per sentence, so a failed rerun can resume precisely at the erroring item rather than start over.
Long-document translation is fed segment-by-segment or section-by-section, and cross-segment consistency is carried by translation memory plus glossary.
Localising a multi-speaker podcast first routes each diarised speaker into their own synthesis channel, then rejoins them along the timeline.
Confidential clients typically require on-premise delivery of batch localization, and any telemetry must be globally disableable.
The glossary import endpoint enforces pack-level domain, source_lang and target_lang to prevent polluting the generic table.
The request's domain field propagates into the TM hit check, so cross-domain memory is refused to avoid context mis-fit.
The distillation worker is asynchronous; when its queue is full it drops candidates rather than blocking the main path -- a deliberate latency-over-completeness choice.
The routing table is data-driven with four precedence levels in order: routes.json < settings.json < environment variables < initialisation JSON.
End-to-end evaluation samples carry sub-domain labels so per-group quality deltas ("pipeline core vs polysemy boundary") can be computed.
The performance metric for batch localisation is sentences per hour; the realtime metric is end-to-end milliseconds -- two completely different scales.
The first-launch experience of a desktop product matters: missing models must surface as a friendly UI message, not an exception.
Local model downloads must show progress and support resumable fetch; hundreds of megabytes to gigabytes are not worth restarting from zero.
Switching between quality and realtime mode happens per request; a single batch can freely mix them without a global toggle.
The sidecar's lifecycle is managed by the UI layer -- health-poll on startup, graceful kill on shutdown.
The contract between the Python sidecar and the Rust core is HTTP over JSON, with native bindings forbidden so no new dependency burden creeps in.
Windows Smart App Control intercepts unsigned locally built DLLs, so product signing must cover every binary shipped.
The licence gate covers Cargo.lock, Python requirements, .NET csproj files, and any third-party material referenced from the docs.
Source-code comments are English throughout; user-facing documentation is Chinese with an English twin linked from the README.
Every externally published number must trace back to a reproducible evaluation run -- script, seed and timestamp included.
Docs must not carry machine drive paths; every default path resolves through data_dir.
Build entry points are Python scripts, with a thin .bat on Windows and .sh on Linux as OS-level wrappers.
The pre-commit hook dispatches different checks by file path and only allows a commit when every applicable check passes.
Conventional Commits is the message convention: type(scope): subject says "what changed in which part" in a single line.
Pathspec-scoped commits are the atomicity guarantee in concurrent collaboration; a shared staging area is easily swept by another session's commit.
Patch files must state their line-ending policy explicitly; under autocrlf=true on Windows a checkout may otherwise break git apply.
"缓存" is cache in a CDN context but buffer in a video buffering context; conflating them misleads the reader.
"编码器" and "加密器" are near-homographs in Chinese but encoder and encryptor in English -- the translation must not slide.
"解码器" and "解密器" are equally confusable: decoder versus decryptor -- decide by compression domain or security domain.
A video keyframe and an animation keyframe share the Chinese term "关键帧" but denote different things.
"声道" and "通道" both render as channel in English; the UI must pick one explicitly by domain rather than default.
"分词" is tokenization in NLP but may be word segmentation in Chinese text processing -- the two operate at different granularity.
"重采样" in audio is resampling; in statistics it is bootstrap sampling -- the surrounding domain decides the rendering.
"预训练" and "预热" both start with "pre-" in Chinese, but they are pretraining and warmup in English -- completely different engineering meanings.
When the decode thread stalls on the queue, flushing the buffer once usually gets the pipeline moving again.
The backpressure signal has to reach the capture side, otherwise the buffer overflows without anyone noticing.
Raising the chunk size from 20 ms to 40 ms increases end-to-end latency but tolerates network jitter better.
When the data stream is duplicated in the middle layer, the frame numbering of both copies must not advance independently.
Whenever a thread pool is introduced anywhere in the pipeline, the ordering guarantee of speech-segment end events must be rechecked.
Queue depth is an observable and should appear in the diagnostics endpoint next to time to first packet.
After streaming recognition is cut off mid-way, the resume position must be recorded in frames rather than in seconds.
When audio is processed in chunks, block boundaries must align to samples, or clicks appear at the seams.
The buffering policy must state whether it drops the oldest frame or blocks the producer; the two semantics differ completely.
The pipeline has to flush every stage before shutdown, otherwise the final speech segment disappears without a trace.
The runtime puts the decoder on a worker thread, leaving the UI thread to only refresh the timeline.
Rebuilding the feature-dimension array every frame causes noticeable garbage-collection pressure; reuse the buffer instead.
An inference service should report its concurrency limit together with queue depth so operators can judge how long the line is.
The reconnect policy for streaming transport must separate a server-initiated close from network jitter.
Segmentation boundaries in voice segmentation depend on the frame shift; changing it also changes segment granularity.
A buffer watermark has to bound latency and frame loss together; an upper limit alone is not enough.
When decoding runs slower than capture, backpressure shifts the waiting onto the encoder queue.
After a checkpoint is loaded, run one warm-up inference before accepting the first request.
Log lines for each pipeline stage should carry the speech-segment id, or cross-segment issues cannot be attributed.
Hot-swapping model weights must freeze the queue and wait for in-flight requests before the replacement.
Long documents that exceed the token limit must be fed paragraph by paragraph rather than truncated as a whole.
Retry limits for subword-segmented languages should be designed around the token budget, not a sentence count.
The runtime offers two memory pools, one for the frame buffer and one for feature tensors.
Chunk boundaries and forced-alignment timestamps must share one sample-based reference.
The first block emitted by streaming synthesis must contain whole frames, or the player goes silent at the start.
If a full queue causes drops, say so in the response instead of letting the caller believe everything was processed.
The decoder's channel order must match the container's declaration; swapped stereo channels are hard to hear.
End-to-end evaluation should time each stage; the gap between capture and inference is often overlooked.
Pinning inference threads reduces jitter but lets the queue build up more easily on single-core machines.
Converting the frame buffer from seconds to bytes needs both the sample rate and the feature dimension.
Merged data streams require renumbering, or downstream stages cannot tell which speech segment ends first.
A flush must be idempotent; calling it twice must not empty the queue twice.
Pipeline monitoring should expose per-stage queue depth and the age of the oldest request.
Language codes stay canonical internally and are localized only at display time, to avoid comparison forks.
Sharing one vocabulary cache between automatic speech recognition and translation saves repeated initialization.
If any stage of the speech loop becomes asynchronous, redefine where time to first playable segment is measured.
A buffer's target watermark should adapt to the historical distribution of network jitter.
The overlap length of chunked processing affects the stability of the spectrum estimate.
Backpressure loses fidelity across a process boundary, so pass the waiting reason explicitly rather than inferring it from timeouts.
On a checkpoint switch, keep the old instance alive until the last request has returned.
Queueing ahead of the pipeline must not count as inference time, or latency reports become misleading.
With a 25 ms frame length and a 10 ms frame shift, one frame spans three sample windows.
A queue wait limit needs a hard ceiling; past the platform's representable range it raises instead of waiting.
The inference batch cap should follow the memory watermark; a fixed value is unstable on shared machines.
Millisecond timestamps from the decoder are enough; subtitle files do not need centiseconds.
Partial results from streaming recognition should say whether they can be revised, so downstream can decide to show them.
Merging voice segmentation with speaker diarization can disagree on boundaries and needs arbitration.
On shutdown the runtime should print what each buffer still holds, to tell later whether data was dropped.
Keep draining after a queue timeout, so a flood of backlogged requests does not overwhelm the restart.
Replace locks with message passing for shared mutable state in a pipeline; a stage reorder otherwise deadlocks.
Time to first packet and time to first playable segment are different measurements and must not share one metric name.
Near the token budget, trim the few-shot examples before the terminology list.
Chunk-downloaded audio needs a sample-rate consistency check before recognition.
Over-prefetching in the buffer lengthens how long the final speech segment waits.
Sequence wrap-around in streaming transport must be defined by the protocol, not left to implementation habit.
On a corrupt frame the decoder should emit silence and record the reason rather than raising.
The inference result cache is keyed by language pair and domain together; reusing across domains mismatches context.
Each stage of the pipeline needs its own degradation path instead of failing as a whole.
Separate queueing time from processing time in runtime metrics so operators know which stage to scale.
Do audio normalization before encoding, or the dynamic range gets compressed away.
Transcoding jobs resume per segment, so a failure does not restart the whole file.
Constant-quality rate control lets the keyframe interval float instead of staying fixed.
A container format stamps media time, so wall-clock conversion needs the track's start offset.
The multiplexer demands sample-aligned tracks, or it inserts filler packets.
Decoder error concealment is configurable; too much makes damaged audio smooth but wrong.
The group of pictures length and the random access points decide how responsive seeking is.
Inter-frame prediction depends on reference order, so a lost frame must trigger a fresh sync point.
A higher quantization parameter compresses more, but blocking artifacts on text edges become visible first.
The entropy coding context table must reset at stream start; reusing it across segments causes drift.
A transcoding chain keeps the original sample count unless resampling is explicitly requested.
Lossless compression guarantees identical bits, not a better subjective quality.
At low bitrate, lossy compression sacrifices high-frequency detail before the voice fundamental.
A demuxer must emit samples in decode order when tracks were interleaved on write.
A bitrate estimation window that is too short mistakes a still frame for something expendable.
Negotiation between codecs must fall back to the lowest configuration both sides support.
Container format limits on track count are hit first when many subtitle languages are bundled.
Keyframe intervals beyond two seconds make scrubbing previews sluggish in live scenarios.
Widening the motion estimation search rarely improves quality and always costs encoding time.
Intra-frame prediction pays off on still imagery more than inter-frame, though the bitrate rises.
The container header an encoder writes must match the real channel count, not the template's.
Save the probe result before transcoding, to check channel and bit-depth changes afterwards.
In multi-process transcoding each process owns its buffer; sharing frame buffer memory is forbidden.
Tracks whose decode order differs from display order need an explicit container offset.
Rate control should allocate less to narration silence than to the speech in the same passage.
Entropy decoding errors recover only at a keyframe; silence is not a sync point.
The first frame of a group of pictures must be independently decodable, or segmenting tools show corruption.
A container format's metadata length limits affect how bilingual subtitle tracks are named.
Pass-through of a subtitle track must still preserve the original timeline precision.
Too much transcoding concurrency makes random disk writes the bottleneck rather than the CPU.
Each line of a bilingual subtitle has to meet the reading-speed bound on its own, not just the Chinese one.
Hard subtitles can only be changed by re-encoding, while soft subtitles let you swap the track at any time.
The subtitle track's encoding must be declared in the container, or the player guesses from the local code page.
Dubbing replaces the audio track, not the duration; squeezing the duration breaks audio-video sync.
Narration is slower paced than dialogue, so dubbing cannot reuse the same timeline strategy.
Lip sync adjustment works only within a couple of frames; beyond that the mismatch becomes visible.
Subtitle timing drift accumulates at paragraph boundaries rather than spreading evenly across lines.
Subtitle alignment should cut points by voice segmentation first, then map text onto those boundaries.
Segment timing must be based on the media timeline; do not assume millisecond values survive transcoding.
Detect and strip a byte order mark on subtitle import, or the first line carries an invisible character.
Dubbing and lip sync have different acceptance criteria: one is judged by the replaced voice, the other by the picture.
Multi-language subtitle releases should key subtitle tracks by language code, not display name.
Line breaking for subtitles belongs to the renderer, decoupled from export options.
A timeline panel must separate video frame numbers from audio sample indices; conversion depends on frame rate and sample rate.
The loudness target of a dubbed audio track must match the original or the level jumps when switching.
Subtitle timing needs remapping under variable playback speed; scaling timestamps is not enough.
Export to SRT needs millisecond timestamps while ASS needs centiseconds.
The preview renders soft subtitles so users can toggle the subtitle track.
Re-muxing is enough when only the timeline changes; do not add an unnecessary second lossy compression pass.
A dubbing delivery needs both the audio track and bilingual subtitles, or the work cannot be reviewed.
Subtitle line breaking must avoid cutting inside words: Chinese by speech segment, Latin scripts by word boundary.
Timeline alignment checks can run before dubbing, without waiting for all assets.
Cutting a line shifts every later timestamp, so reflow the whole block rather than nudging rows.
Dubbing sessions must keep a consistent sample rate and bit depth to avoid a second resampling.
The maximum characters per subtitle line should be measured for the target language's width, not copied from Chinese habits.
The audio-video sync tolerance depends on the material; conference recordings and animation differ.
Run punctuation restoration after segmentation, or punctuation crosses into neighbouring lines.
When narration and dialogue share one audio track, dubbing replacement must locate speech segments first.
Compare sample rate, channel count and bit depth before export, and refuse a silent resample when they differ.
Subtitle timing on material with dropped frames must interpolate by real frame durations, not a constant rate.
Multi-speaker podcast localization needs speaker diarization first, then timbre assignment per role.
Use decoded frames as the lip-sync reference; pre-encode frame order is unreliable.
When a subtitle track carries rich text, spell out the container's tag whitelist.
Converting hard subtitles into soft ones needs optical recognition, and quality drops below authored text.
Squeezing duration to 95 percent accumulates into a half-second offset; long material needs segment-wise correction.
Dubbing delivery ships two artefacts, a timeline file and an audio track, with naming agreed in advance.
Line length and lines per screen together bound the readable speed of subtitles.
Subtitle tracks in several languages should share one segmentation result to avoid drifting apart.
Timeline export must state whether it references media time or wall-clock time.
Keep or remove dubbing breathes consistently with the source; over-cleaning sounds artificial.
On alignment failure, report the speech segment ids that disagree so a human can step in.
Record the start offsets of subtitle and video tracks separately; they cannot be recovered after muxing.
Declare speed and pitch changes separately; changing one alone sounds inconsistent.
In dialogue-dense material, break subtitles on pauses rather than punctuation.
After swapping an audio track, preserve its language tag and default-track flag when re-muxing.
Let intelligibility rule subtitle timing fixes rather than aligning to frames.
When a dubbing runs long against the picture, adjust pacing before cutting content.
Stacking bilingual subtitles doubles lines per screen, so dwell time must shrink.
The player drops subtitle lines whose timestamps precede the video's start offset.
Check for overlapping subtitles before export; overlap leaves the reader unsure which line to read.
Leave silence padding between dubbing takes to make later alignment possible.
Split subtitles for long video by semantic paragraphs rather than fixed seconds.
For non-integer frame rates, export subtitle timing in a timebase instead of frame counts.
Set the loudness target before muxing; compressing afterwards also affects dialogue.
Line breaking needs protection rules for abbreviations and proper names.
Normalize differing frame rates onto one timeline before any lip-sync work.
Ellipses and dashes in subtitles need their own pause rules for speech synthesis.
Review dubbing speech segment by segment; one pass over the whole show misses local desync.
In multilingual delivery, subtitle filenames carry the language code and provenance.
Timeline export must include track offsets and the effective frame rate.
A subtitle line's minimum dwell time is set by its length, not by cut rhythm.
When narration shares a track with music, dubbing needs an extra separation step.
Record the subtitle track's character set in the export log for field debugging.
Measure audio-video sync on audible events such as claps and strikes.
Spot-check subtitle alignment at the start, middle and end; the middle drifts most.
Dubbing must re-break lines for the target language; literal length usually exceeds the picture.
Log dropped-frame positions on subtitle timelines, or corrections have no basis.
Write default and forced track flags explicitly when merging tracks.
When exporting bilingual subtitles, dwell time must satisfy one reading-speed bound for both languages.
Underrun in the buffer makes whole subtitle lines flicker, independent of content.
Export the segment timing sheet as a checklist dubbing sessions can follow line by line.
Keep numbering for silent spans on the timeline; deleting one shifts everything after it.
Replacing an audio track must update its duration metadata, not just the payload.
A high cache hit rate does not mean good translation quality; it only means the sentence was seen before.
The cache here is the audio frame cache, not a web cache.
An over-large prefetch cache lengthens how long the last speech segment waits.
A disk cache survives power loss, so its invalidation policy needs a version tag.
A cache key must include the language pair and domain, or cross-domain reuse mismatches context.
After a codec upgrade old streams still decode, and the compatibility matrix belongs in the release notes.
This file needs the software codec rather than the system one.
Codec negotiation must look at what both sides support, not just one's own advertised list.
A wrong container format can drop the subtitle track even while video looks fine.
Re-muxing the same material into another container can shift duration metadata by a few milliseconds.
A mix should treat dialogue, music and effects as separate stems rather than stacked on one track.
Set the loudness target of the mix to the delivery platform's requirement, not as loud as possible.
Leave headroom during mixing so later compression does not lift the noise floor.
Exported stems must share one start time, or reassembling them misaligns.
Accompaniment left in the dry vocal is recognised as speech and invents transcript content.
Keep the dubbing dry vocal archived on its own; after the mix it can no longer be separated.
Map channels in the track table according to the container, not the player's display order.
Rerun the coverage matrix after any change to the routing table, or the report describes a stale configuration.
The routing here means engine selection, not a network routing table.
Video clip cuts must land on keyframes, or the result decodes as corrupted.
Splitting tools handle long groups of pictures poorly without re-encoding first.
Spreadsheet data used as subtitle source needs formula-injection guards on both sides.
Keep the table schema backward compatible; old assets without the new field are the norm.
Frame rate is a media property, not an editing one; do not convert it twice on export.
If part of the model weights are corrupt, refuse the checkpoint instead of silently filling in.
On a vocabulary mismatch, subword tokenisation degrades to per-character and quality collapses.
Whether the pipeline rejects or waits on a full queue has to be observable.
A queue policy change moves time to first packet, so keep it in the same regression.
Buffering and buffer both read the same in Chinese; keep one English word for them.
Separate the data stream from streaming transport in docs: one is content, the other is how it travels.
Say from context whether checkpoint means model weights or a resume point.
A pipeline stage and a speech segment are different notions; name them apart.
Channel covers both senses in English, so the Chinese interface must pick one by domain instead of defaulting.
A keyframe is not the first frame; preview logic must decide on decodable frames.
Tokens and subwords differ for billing and for segmentation; do not share one metric.
Keep bitrate and rate control terminology distinct but consistent, or readers see two metrics.
Chunking is not batching; chunking is a window inside streaming, batching is a group of requests.
Declare resampling and requantisation separately, or one hides the other's damage.
Error out when an encoder preset and an explicit quality flag conflict, rather than silently choosing one.
Record why the decoder fell back from hardware to software.
When container and codec capabilities disagree, the container's constraint wins.
Warn before failing when the mix exceeds the container's track limit.
Dry vocal quality drives dubbing intelligibility; leftover accompaniment shows up as transcript errors.
Report the row when a subtitle sheet lacks a duration column, instead of defaulting it.
Renumber frames after concatenation, or decode order and display order invert.
Routing decisions must be readable back; a result without a path cannot be debugged.
A vocabulary's domain coverage decides subword quality; a general one shreds proper nouns.
Expire the cache per language pair across versions rather than clearing it globally.
Automatic evaluation cannot replace human review; on narrow domains the correlation drops sharply.
Sample evaluations must stratify by sub-domain, or the long tail stays invisible.
Chinese speech evaluation leans on character error rate, because word error rate depends on a tokenisation convention.
Change one variable per experiment; altering injection policy and its cap together makes results unattributable.
Evaluation samples should cover short, medium and long sentences; their failure modes differ.
Once a regression baseline is published, fix the seed and timestamp so it can be reproduced.
Statistical significance is not practical significance; check whether a few tenths reach the user.
Report negative results under the same discipline, not only the round that worked.
Scope a rerun explicitly, or it overwrites evidence belonging to other engines.
Do not leave failed rows in the denominator, or an engine fault reads as a quality drop.
Same-arm repeats give the noise floor; a delta inside it cannot be attributed.
Every metric row must trace to one reproducible run with script, samples and time.
Keep a measured source for spot-checked contamination rates instead of reusing an old estimate.
Keep evaluation scripts under version control; a one-off script outside the repo cannot be re-checked.
Pair on the same sentences; a difference between unlike windows is not an effect.
Hold-out means held out; samples that took part in tuning cannot be the evaluation set.
State limitations plainly; do not extrapolate a distribution beyond the domains it covers.
Every published number needs a measured source; label estimates as estimates.
Give repeated runs of one metric distinct identities so a new run cannot replace the old evidence.
Evaluation corpora must pass the license gate; no copyleft licence may enter the artefacts.
Record sub-domain group boundaries in config, not in someone's memory of line numbers.
Absolute automatic scores make poor promises; relative deltas plus human review hold.
Refuse to conclude under an insufficient sample rather than printing an optimistic number.
Fail non-zero in evaluation scripts; a silent pass misleads everything downstream.
Never reorder existing rows when growing a regression set, or past numbers stop being comparable.
Land a metric-definition change in its own commit, not together with corpus growth.
State the evaluation environment up front; whether a host service is running changes the conclusion.
Write the annotation guideline before human review, or hindsight biases the labels.
Control and treatment need the same machine and build; cross-machine comparison is void.
Rule out the measurement layer before blaming the model when a metric drops.
Reproducibility needs data, script, timestamp and environment; one missing voids it.
Verify self-describing fields before storing evidence; anonymous artefacts cannot be cited.
Report the confidence interval with the sample size; a bare mean carries no information.
Build boundary probes around ambiguous words; random sampling cannot measure domain sensitivity.
Classified batch localization normally runs on site, with telemetry and any outbound call closeable as a whole.
In an offline delivery bundle, neither models nor vocabularies may rely on runtime downloads.
Privacy mode closes the upgrade path rather than sending source text to the cloud.
Logs must not carry source fragments; the user's material is itself sensitive.
Check metadata on export: track titles and author fields often leak project names.
Audit records keep the basis of a decision, never the translated text.
Before handing deliverables to a third party, confirm the glossary holds no internal codenames.
Cutting network access has to work as one configuration switch, not per call site.
Clearing the translation memory must also clear term rows derived from it, or they keep resurfacing.
A site machine has no outbound access, so the media must carry every dependency and its checksum.
A rollback must cover the glossary and memory schema, or an old build misreads a new format.
The licence audit must cover dependencies that appear only at runtime, not just the build manifest.
Keep separate memory stores per project for sensitive material, to avoid cross-project bleed.
The diagnostics endpoint must stay available under privacy mode and issue no requests itself.
After disabling cloud engines, still state plainly that the result is local rather than implying an upgrade.
On-site log levels should be raised temporarily and revert, rather than emitting detail forever.
A pre-delivery checklist must cover consistency of font, encoding and language code.
Record resume progress per segment for batch jobs, without keeping the source text.
Until access control exists, the exposed service surface must stay loopback-only.
A published domain glossary must not carry a user's private rows.
Export bundles should ship a manifest so the customer can check for sensitive fields.
Telemetry off by default is safer than on by default with a switch; the cost of a mistake is asymmetric.
Use synthetic material in docs, never a client's real transcript.
Keep keys and tokens out of the repository, including fake ones used in tests.
Clearing data must return a verifiable receipt rather than a bare success.
Verify model provenance in offline environments, so the download path does not become the attack surface.
The metadata fields of exported files are the most overlooked compliance item.
Reproduce field issues with a minimal sample, so a whole batch never leaves the site.
Until roles exist, isolate memory stores by directory on shared terminals.
Align audit retention with the delivery contract instead of keeping records by default.
Separate logical flagging from physical erasure in delete operations.
Sensitive-domain evaluation samples cannot appear in a public benchmark.
Exclude and re-check the user data directory from packaged artefacts before delivery.
On project handover, sign the scope for the glossary and the memory store separately.
Privacy invariants held by a test are worth more than privacy promises in a document.
Disable a feature visibly, rather than hiding its entry point.
A customer's proxy changes probe results, so separate unreachable from unconfigured.
Ship the conservative default and require an explicit action to loosen it.
Leave no partial file behind on export failure: write to a staging name then rename.
Staging names must be unique; a fixed one deletes the wrong file under concurrency.
Run a disconnected drill of first install to prove no hidden outbound requests exist.
Integrity checking of model files should cover both size and digest.
A field upgrade must be able to roll back config alone, keeping confirmed terms.
Logs must name the layer a decision came from, not just the outcome.
When data may not leave the country, cloud engines stay out of routing even if reachable.
Record the delivery package's digest with its version to trace what was installed.
After clearing the memory, term harvesting can still refill it, so allow closing that path.
Rows a user confirmed can be marked non-redistributable.
Internal build artefacts need full signing, or platform security blocks loading.
Judge delivery risk by where data flows, not by how many features exist.
An offline bundle ships the engine, models, vocabularies and a checksum manifest together.
The installer must work into a user directory without elevation.
Sign every build artefact, or platform application control blocks local loading.
Handle non-Latin characters in the install path when loading the native library.
An upgrade keeps the user data directory and migrates its schema version.
Ask what to do with the data directory on uninstall; never delete by default.
The packaging step should enumerate included binaries, so nothing is unsigned or missing.
Keep one config resolution rule across portable and installed forms, or behaviour forks.
Pin runtime dependency versions; a floating minor version can change inference output.
When build and dev machines differ, the clean reproducible one is authoritative.
State which models must be fetched separately, so a gap is not found after installation.
Attribute growth in package size to a specific addition, rather than reporting a total.
A post-install self-check must cover loading the native library and reading a model.
With no outbound access, the update path must accept an offline patch package.
Until hot reload exists, document which layer a config change restarts.
Crash reports collect location and version by default, never the text.
Exclude cache directories when packaging, so a developer's memory store is never shipped.
Keep one thin launcher per OS and put the logic in the shared script.
The build script must skip a stage whose inputs are unchanged by digest.
A clean build must be reproducible with one command, without relying on prior artefacts.
Show the same version string in the UI and in diagnostics.
Ship a licence matrix covering every third-party component in the package.
Split model downloads per language pair so a first install fetches nothing wholesale.
Keep install scripting idempotent; running it twice must not create a second config.
On failure leave a readable reason file, not just a dialog.
Prefer extra diagnostics when debugging in the field, rather than editing code to reproduce it.
Checksum every stage of the packaging chain so a truncated file never reaches an installer.
Map each delivered version to a repository commit for later tracing.
Keep the default data-directory rule identical across bindings.
Reproducing an issue across machines needs version, config and a model inventory.
Verify installer integrity before copying any file.
Include the reproduction command so a customer can rerun measurements alone.
Separate development and release output paths in packaging.
Allow installing docs and tools without models where there is no network.
Write packaged scripts byte-wise; text mode sneaks platform line endings in.
A release build must be reproducible to the same hash in CI, or a version number means little.
The first launch after install needs a guided path, not an empty window.
Keep the installer's version equal to what the runtime self-check reports.
Ship the lockfile with the package so a site can re-check dependencies.
Keep build output separate from sources so binaries never get committed.
Back up the data directory before a field upgrade so a failure restores wholesale.
Strip test fixtures and dev scripts from packaging, cutting size and misuse.
Keep commands in delivered docs matching real entry points, or they rot.
A failing build step must exit non-zero and name the stage.
Release notes must state which behaviour changes break comparison with prior metrics.
Package size is bounded by first-install network conditions, not by feature count.
Read field differences back through diagnostics instead of inferring them from chat.
Artefacts must be verifiable offline on the target machine.
Rebuild only what a change affects, to keep packaging feedback short.
State the deprecation window in compatibility promises so customers can plan.
A release manifest lists each file's provenance and licence.
Detect missing dependencies at install and print the exact fetch command.
Ship dependencies whole where a site has no package manager.
Separate building from publishing so developer credentials never enter artefacts.
Fix environment variables and path normalization for reproducible builds.
Include one clean-machine install in acceptance, or it only works on a dev box.
Leave no absolute default paths inside a package, or another machine cannot reproduce.
Run the full gate before release and record its last line in the ledger.
Store the checksum beside the media so an archive stays verifiable years later.
Test the upgrade path from the oldest delivered version in one hop.
Practise the rollback before delivery; the first attempt should not be a customer's.
Bundle third-party licence texts alongside the packaged material.
Pin toolchain minor versions so optimisation differences do not move performance numbers.
Allow verifying one file at a time, to localise corruption.
Make post-install self-check output copyable to support, with versions and path digests.
Ship documentation readable offline for disconnected environments.
Confirm sample corpora are public-safe, never client material.
Archive release build logs to reconstruct the environment and outcome later.
A delivered version must answer which release introduced a feature.
Allow pointing the build cache outside the tree, to keep the working copy clean.
Run static checks before packaging to catch obvious UI and text faults.
Print the installed components and versions at the end of setup.
Report the single missing dependency, rather than failing the whole install.
Reproducibility means someone else can run it, not that this machine can.
Sharing one GPU between inference and synthesis starves both; measure them in separate phases.
Near the memory ceiling the system starts paging, and latency grows a long tail.
Thermal throttling slows the same model late in a sustained load, which a short test will miss.
Battery power caps throughput on laptops, so note the power state next to any number.
Judge batch throughput against the memory it costs, or it is only buying time with resources.
Loading a model from disk belongs to first-start wait, not to inference time.
The same weights can behave differently across optimisation levels, so pin the build for regressions.
Repeat a measurement to tell sampling noise from a real change; one run cannot decide.
A shift in hit rate moves latency and cost together, so read them as a pair.
On a slow disk, queueing under concurrent IO reads as a slower model.
Report a distribution for a regression, not a mean; the tail is where the experience lives.
Judge quantisation loss on task metrics, not on weight error alone.
Multi-core scaling stalls at queue synchronisation points, so the speedup curve is not linear.
The first request carries lazy initialisation, so report cold start separately.
Distinguish cache residency from a real leak when memory grows over time.
Paging and garbage collection create spikes, so confirm with a repeat sample.
The same workload takes different times in different orders, so fix input order in a benchmark.
Attach the environment to any performance promise: chip, power state and concurrency.
Overlapping inference with pre-processing makes a segment look shorter than the sum, not faster.
A smaller model can beat a larger one where latency matters; check the quality cost per task.
Size cache prefetch to corpus repetition; on random input prefetch is pure overhead.
If concurrent decoding does not raise throughput, the bottleneck is compute rather than scheduling.
Under rising load the tail degrades before the median, so read the pair together.
Mixing waiting and processing into one metric hides queue build-up.
Benchmark on hardware in the delivered class, or the numbers mean nothing.
A memory ceiling protects a long run better than a larger cache does.
Threaded decoding adds context switches and is slower on short text.
Acceleration pays off only as far as operators are covered, so measure it.
Stamp performance baselines with collection time; background jobs disturb day-to-day comparison.
An acceleration switch that is not wired up must not be reported as a gain.
Feeding long text segment by segment raises total time but lowers peak memory.
Report quantised and full-precision variants of one model as separate rows.
Note resident services holding memory before measuring; they change the result.
Check performance and quality together, so "faster and equal" is defensible.
Alert on queue age rather than queue depth, since depth alone is not user-visible harm.
Do not divide a batch's total by its count and call it single-request latency.
Give both cold-load and warm latency; reporting one flatters the number.
Driver updates change kernel behaviour, so record the driver version with a result.
Set concurrency ceilings from measured resources, not from a core count.
Keep throughput and latency as two goals in reports; they pull apart.
Progress for long jobs should reflect completed work, not a fixed tick.
Attribute a metric's improvement to a specific change, or it is just noise.
Log writing by the harness itself can slow the service under test.
Give a regression a decided threshold with a noise band around it.
Features must run on machines without accelerators, not on a specific kernel.
Judge a cache by hit rate and invalidation cost together.
When start-up cost dominates short tasks, optimising the algorithm returns little.
The gain from one to two concurrent workers is usually smaller than from two to three.
Cite an unreproducible performance number only as a historical fact.
Confirm nothing else is running on the device before measuring.
Paging produces minute-scale spikes in tail latency; do not call it model decay.
Label a hardware switch honestly when it is not actually wired up.
Separate per-item cost from amortised cost in performance measurement.
An optimisation whose gain is eaten by extra waiting nets out to zero.
Measure long-run stability by exit codes and error counts, not by optimism.
A report's conclusion should point to data reproducible on the same machine.
Record performance-versus-quality choices as product decisions, not technical defaults.
Energy becomes a constraint in long batch inference, not only in mobile use.
An unpinned build makes two measurements incomparable.
Stating device scope inside a conclusion beats a disclaimer elsewhere.
Schedule measurement windows away from system updates and index rebuilds.
Test an accelerated path for correctness separately from its speed.
A performance defect needs a re-runnable entry point for regression checks.
Higher batch throughput is not lower per-item latency; accept them separately.
When a metric's jitter exceeds the version difference, it cannot support a conclusion.
The flywheel assumes the AI got it right once, so local reuse is what saves afterwards.
The distillation threshold sets the glossary's signal-to-noise ratio; too loose pollutes every later request.
Machine-extracted rows must be distinguishable from human-confirmed ones, or review is pointless.
One term may translate differently per domain, so the key must include the domain.
A user's edit outranks any automatic source and must not be silently overwritten.
A glossary must be exportable for review, without the user's private rows.
Fragmented distillation output has to be stopped by a source-language check.
Validate the flywheel with both hit rate and quality; either alone misleads.
A mistranslation stored in memory is reused forever, so the bad-mark path must work.
Glossary size is not injection size; only the rows reaching context have effect.
When the injection window sorts by confidence, ties need a deterministic order, or runs are irreproducible.
Read back whether a term applied from the response, do not guess it.
Returned distillation rows must be switchable off wholesale, to isolate faults.
Glossary changes must surface in a re-run, or a semantic change stays invisible.
The cost of one wrong term is that every sentence containing it reuses it.
Glossary governance is traceability: source, confidence and editor must be distinguishable.
Locking and deleting are different protections and cannot substitute for each other.
Maintain glossaries per language pair; reusing across pairs bleeds meaning.
Test a new domain pack on the same sentences, never across batches.
Reviewed rows should record which batch they came from, to allow reversal.
Term conflicts must name both provenances rather than silently picking one.
Rerun the quality regression after changing the distillation threshold, or the ratio is unknown.
Escape rules are needed when a term's translation contains a placeholder.
The flywheel's ceiling is set by how repetitive the content is, not by the model.
Allow replacing a whole domain glossary so a customer's own terms can ship.
Separate "engine cannot use terms" from "no term matched this sentence".
State whether a denominator is all rows or only rows containing terms.
Roll back the glossary and the memory store independently.
A distillation batch window affects insert order, so cross-batch comparison needs care.
A locked row stays editable by its own author; a lock is not a permission wall.
Governance shows up in consistency metrics, not in row counts.
Measure a pack's coverage against the corpus, do not infer it from row counts.
At cold start, a few hand-picked high-value terms beat bulk import.
Where two renderings coexist, choose explicitly and record why.
The flywheel assumes correct renderings get reused; reusing wrong ones amplifies them.
Keep a pack's licence and provenance traceable, and exclude copyleft sources.
Allow bulk cleanup by provenance, to undo one bad import.
Refuse a partially failed import whole, leaving no half-applied rows.
Do not raise a declared confidence during import; the authored value is final.
Deleting a domain row needs the full key, or it hits a generic twin instead.
Keep glossary review in step with corpus growth, or metrics drift.
Keep a retired term for history while excluding it from injection.
Layer glossaries by scope: generic, domain and project.
Handle polysemy with context, not by forcing one rendering.
Include the upgrade rate in flywheel metrics; frequent upgrades mean local capability is thin.
A single-arm measurement has nothing to compare against.
Injection and memory reuse are two paths; attribute their effects separately.
Pack review must point to specific row changes.
Handle term conflicts conservatively: report ambiguity instead of rewriting silently.
Automatic rows need a lower default confidence than human-confirmed ones.
Record glossary changes with the measurement identity, to explain numbers later.
Judge an import by rows written, not rows supplied.
If a pack shows no gain, check first whether injection actually arrived.
Glossary governance yields a reusable domain asset, not one-off configuration.
Every link of the flywheel needs an observable, or decay cannot be located.
Unlocking a row must be traceable to an actor.
A term's translation length affects layout and dwell time.
Roll back exactly the batch a change introduced.
Keep the evaluation term set equal to the delivered one, or the conclusion does not apply.
A pack stays maintainable when row granularity is even.
Avoid accumulating unevidenced rows; retire low-hit ones periodically.
An accepted mistranslation is amplified by the flywheel, so gate it earlier.
The worth of a glossary is the consistency a customer can perceive.
Show which engine actually answered, so users can tell which tier produced the result.
State the reason and the next step in plain words, not only an error code.
Keep the copy button reachable before a panel collapses.
Every status change needs a text form, not colour alone.
Let users resize and keep column widths, as the glossary gains columns over versions.
Keep keyboard focus in the input area after a translation, for continuous work.
A progress bar must be cancellable, and say the native call still drains afterwards.
Write errors without internal jargon; translate internal names into plain language.
Use the same wording across UI and help, or one feature gets two names.
Screen readers need an explicit label and role on every status line.
Do not put default focus on a button that triggers a network call.
A batch completion notice must give both succeeded and failed counts.
Keep table sorting stable; equal-valued rows must not reshuffle on refresh.
Explain a disabled control, or a greyed-out one reads as a defect.
The first-launch missing-model notice should carry a copyable fetch command.
Let the UI show only user-confirmed rows, to make provenance checkable.
Reset scroll position when switching language pairs in a long list.
State the paste length limit and what happens beyond it.
Pull numbers in messages from live config, never hardcode them.
Cancelling must not lose finished work, only what was still in flight.
Wrap cell text so long terms stay fully visible.
A transient status message should be replaced, not queued behind others.
Spell out blast radius and reversibility in a destructive dialog.
The interface must stay responsive while waiting on an external service.
Include contrast and focus order in accessibility checks.
Show a metric to its measurement precision, no further.
Let users copy the full context of an error for support.
Filter the table by source, to view machine and human rows apart.
An empty state needs a next step, never a blank pane.
Button labels should name the outcome, not the mechanism.
Keep interface language and content language distinct.
Advanced settings must say when they apply: immediately or after restart.
Keep feedback beside the control that produced it.
Mark the active sort column in the header.
A delete confirmation must show how many rows are involved.
Name the missing precondition for an unavailable feature.
Suggest splitting on over-long input, rather than truncating quietly.
Show the routing that is actually in force, not what configuration intended.
Avoid framing a technical limitation as the user's mistake.
Import results must separate rows written from rows supplied.
Keep engine names equal to their registered identifiers.
Preserve the current selection across refreshes unless it no longer exists.
Change UI text and docs in one commit, or they diverge.
Pick shortcuts that do not collide with input-method bindings.
Copyable paths in messages, without a personal directory inside.
Show a completed count instead of a percentage that cannot be measured.
Keep one name for a state across UI, logs and diagnostics.
Reassure the user that work continues during a long wait.
Label a switch by what it controls, not by a hoped-for effect.
Show the provenance column so review has a starting point.
Offer the exact command to recover, not generic advice to retry.
Preview a pack's contents read-only before import.
Format numbers with explicit units to avoid ambiguity.
Show an edit marker on rows the user changed.
Timestamp state transitions on long-running tasks.
Pinpoint the row number for a batch failure.
Keep UI copy from promising a quality tier it cannot guarantee.
Separate waiting on an external service from local computation.
Let UI copy be proofread without a code change.
Copied diagnostics must include version and environment.
Label a confidence column with what it measures.
Mark scope when a feature is not present on every platform.
Offer a simplified view that hides internal columns.
Estimate expected duration for slow operations rather than spinning.
Use placeholders in example paths, never a real directory.
Make an operation's effect readable back for confirmation.
Show a disabled control together with its reason.
Let a UI filter show only the failed rows of a batch.
Keep status messages free of internal shorthand.
Keep a long table's header pinned while scrolling.
Present a number under the same definition as its source.
The interface must work fully with no network access.
Keep stated defaults drawn from the same source as real defaults.
A commit subject names the part of the system a change touches.
Run the lookalike scan after writing prose; do not trust the eye.
Attach evidence to a ledger status change: a test name or a command.
Append corrections to dated entries; never rewrite them.
Keep one change to one kind of problem, or attribution dies.
Use path-scoped commits with concurrent sessions, so shared staging cannot sweep another's work.
Provide one thin launcher per OS and keep the logic in a single place.
Register a new suite in both the local gate and the pipeline.
Promote a one-off probe's finding into a re-runnable entry point, or nobody can re-check it.
Tests must not depend on a developer's running services.
The gate beats eyeballing: it reports assumptions you forgot to update.
An assertion that cannot fail is not an assertion.
Docs are not an implementation basis; code and tests are current truth.
Declare a metric definition change inside the report.
Remove the workaround that masked a defect when fixing it.
State what a measurement cannot prove.
Line endings come from repository attributes, not local configuration.
Keep source comments in English and user-facing copy in the product language.
Trace every published number to one reproducible run.
Self-check convention compliance before review, not after being told.
Search out stale promises after a change, so no second copy survives.
Trust code over the ledger on conflict, then correct the ledger.
Confirm a run's scope and what it can overwrite before starting it.
Keep documented commands executable, never pseudocode.
Print a skip reason; a skip is not a pass.
Bind an explanation to concrete evidence, not to recollection.
Keep one source of truth for a shared counter, or two disagree.
Give a new knob bounds and an injection seam so tests can pin it.
State what a change deliberately did not do, in its message.
Keep a defect's sub-item numbering consistent, or work split by number misaligns.
Register a re-runnable entry in the intent table, not in chat history.
Commit an evaluation's inputs with its script, to allow replay.
Note environment preconditions; they are not optional in a cross-machine citation.
Cite things other people can actually run.
After a fix, check for the same shape elsewhere in the tree.
The voice loop chains segmentation, recognition, translation and synthesis in series.
Recognition errors propagate into translation and synthesis, so score segments independently.
Excessive microphone gain pushes the waveform into clipping and ruins recognisability.
Trim silence after segmentation, not by a blanket energy threshold on the waveform.
Normalise speaker output volume before encoding, not after.
Flush the buffer when the playhead crosses a segment, or the previous audio lingers.
Route multiple speakers apart before assigning synthesis channels.
Set the voice model size ceiling by first-install download conditions.
A transcript plus translation in a demo must not be called simultaneous interpretation.
Switch timbre at segment boundaries, not mid-segment.
Share one set of language codes between recognition and translation, or the two diverge.
Match voice material's sample rate to what the model expects.
A segmentation change moves both intelligibility and latency, so judge them together.
Keep the text result when synthesis fails, rather than losing the transcript too.
Voice evaluation samples must cover both noisy and accented conditions.
A vocoder bounds naturalness, and not in lockstep with model size.
Language detection is unstable on mixed Chinese-English material, so fall back per segment.
Int8 quantisation shrinks size; measure the naturalness cost per task.
Rerun end-to-end metrics whenever any single model in the chain changes.
Allow delivery of transcript and subtitles without forcing synthesis.
Let an offline voice package update recognition or synthesis independently.
Speaker diarization merges two voices on overlap, so keep human review.
Forced-alignment timestamp accuracy decides subtitle usability and dubbing placement.
Inverse text normalization turns spoken numbers into written form, which downstream parsing depends on.
A thin vocabulary shreds proper nouns and drags recognition down.
Half-precision inference is unsupported on some hardware, so keep a fallback.
Deliver a model's provenance and licence with the weights, not the file alone.
Until streaming synthesis lands, voice product copy promises transcript and translation only.
Keep voice evaluation off the device a resident service is using.
Flag low-confidence segments in subtitles rather than guessing silently.
Make resampling and filter choice explicit in audio format conversion.
Voice model updates land off the product release cycle, so allow independent upgrade.
Export transcript and translation separately for different downstream needs.
Match synthesis pace and subtitle dwell to one reading constraint.
Measure voice material by active speech span, not file length.
Make each stage's latency measurable, or optimisation has no basis.
Balance model size against time-to-first-use by product scenario.
Accept voice delivery on the target machine, not the development box.
Failures in the voice loop must name the failing stage, not the loop as a whole.
Extend recognition vocabularies per customer domain instead of patching a model.
Keep synthesis parameters so the same result can be reproduced.
Show voice quality and latency side by side in one report.
List each model's licence, size and fetch method in the manifest.
Keep voice evaluation corpora licence-compatible with the product.
Instrument start and end times for every stage of the chain.
Declare recognition and synthesis language support separately, not as one list.
On a model fetch failure, print the copyable command and the expected digest.
A request should read back the path it took, the time each stage spent and the engine that answered.
A health check should separate alive, ready and busy rather than collapsing them.
Requests waiting in a queue need a visible metric, or operators can only infer them from timing.
A wait limit needs an upper bound; past the representable range it severs instead of waiting.
Record abandoned requests, since they still consumed a real computation.
A suggested wait should come from a quantile of finished work, not from an average.
A bounded restart must be triggerable on purpose, not only by accident.
Scope an evaluation run by pair and engine, so evidence belonging elsewhere survives.
Close a batch with a summary: counts, failures, the latency distribution and an exit code.
Observability data must separate its own cost from the measured system's cost.
Leave a minimal replayable input for every reproduced failure.
Name metrics with unit and definition, or one label carries two meanings.
Long-run alerts should key off trend and noise band, not a single point.
Judge a change on the same machine and load, comparing before and after.
A rerun must land under the same identity name, for traceability across versions.
Aggregate logs by request identity, not only by time order.
Absolute metric values are not comparable across environments; cite the relative difference.
Keep observability fields backward compatible and say what an unknown means.
Persist failed rows separately, so a rerun does not repeat what already succeeded.
A process is observable when a fault can be localised to a stage.
Report the denominator; unanswered rows must not be scored as low quality.
Log chip, memory, concurrency and resident services for every run.
Present jitter as a noise band, not as two numbers to compare.
State which metrics indicate a need to scale, in the operations runbook.
After a fix, confirm the workaround that hid it is gone.
Sample order can fake degradation; revisit the same rows to tell them apart.
Do not claim an unmeasured step has been optimised.
Sample metrics finer than the timescale on which decay appears.
Acceptance of a delivery includes one end-to-end reproducible command.
An evaluation report names the pairs and domains it does not cover.
State in the body when a conclusion depends on some service running.
Observe waiting time as well as processing time.
An incident record holds version, config and an input digest.
Update code and docs together when a metric definition changes.
Keep batch logs safe to publish, without client text.
Fix the control group to one build and one data directory.
Every reported number should point back to its evidence artefact.
Distinguish user-visible waiting from internal waiting in operations metrics.
Give every threshold a source instead of inheriting the previous number.
Clean up resident services after a run, so the next measurement is not polluted.
State what an incident conclusion ruled out, not only what it found.
Trace every percentage in a report back to its counts.
Mark an unreplicated difference as a single observation.
Evaluation scripts must run unchanged on someone else's machine.
A follow-up visit reuses the same identity name so the two runs compare.
Report a missing observation instead of defaulting it.
Decide a metric's purpose when it is collected, not afterwards.
State the rule that refuses a conclusion when the sample is too small.
An evaluation is trustworthy because it could have failed.
